    {
      "id": "UXxU6z4nDWtjt45CC7nuP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1559.8330434163408,
      "y": 1029.3567708333333,
      "width": 430.2896423339844,
      "height": 160,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1937384595,
      "version": 787,
      "versionNonce": 637624125,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777243154739,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We do not have a strong preference on whether this\nwould be a SQL or a NoSQL database.\nRedis is fast, but it is in memory and expensive.\nFor a long term solution, we will store user meta data,\nfull profiles and their score history.\nThis would be a transactional log of every \nscore update, and this allows us to build the \nleaderboard if Redis ever crashes.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We do not have a strong preference on whether this\nwould be a SQL or a NoSQL database.\nRedis is fast, but it is in memory and expensive.\nFor a long term solution, we will store user meta data,\nfull profiles and their score history.\nThis would be a transactional log of every \nscore update, and this allows us to build the \nleaderboard if Redis ever crashes.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "fOJqjk-bDkPQFwzJBRser",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2209.068211873372,
      "y": 1003.4817708333333,
      "width": 487.7920837402344,
      "height": 240,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#c2255c",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 80924915,
      "version": 843,
      "versionNonce": 470471699,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777243867102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q : How would this be a transactional log\nof every score update?\n\nIn such systems, what is the time for REST calls?\nCan REST calls itself take time so that it might be\nconsidered \"slow\" for a system handling 10M users?\n\nIn the Fixed Range partitioning approach, why would we have\nall players in the 0 - 1000 range?\n-- Shouldn't the ranges be dynamic?\nIf we do so, as more and more players improve, can we combine\nshards and reshard them to distribute evenly?",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q : How would this be a transactional log\nof every score update?\n\nIn such systems, what is the time for REST calls?\nCan REST calls itself take time so that it might be\nconsidered \"slow\" for a system handling 10M users?\n\nIn the Fixed Range partitioning approach, why would we have\nall players in the 0 - 1000 range?\n-- Shouldn't the ranges be dynamic?\nIf we do so, as more and more players improve, can we combine\nshards and reshard them to distribute evenly?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "c5UZEZLMZxph92WulLw0B",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 17.875,
      "y": 1707.625,
      "width": 275.3600158691406,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 923874045,
      "version": 90,
      "versionNonce": 1725128317,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777243512054,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Redis ZSET Implementation",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Redis ZSET Implementation",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "JPSkl64BKEDILqodw90uH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20.875,
      "y": 1759.359375,
      "width": 1022.8162841796875,
      "height": 280,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 444476829,
      "version": 1166,
      "versionNonce": 20743901,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777243512054,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Redis in the ZSET implementation uses a combination of HashMap and a skip list.\nFor direct lookups of a player and their score, we can use the Hashmap and answer their score.\n\nBut to identify the rank, we need to do sorting and find the K th largest element. To prevent compute on the go, Redis ZSET \ninternally uses Skip Lists.\n\nA skip list would maintain the elements with three lanes : \n\nLevel 3 : Head -> ..........................................................  -> {\"Carl\" : 500} -> Null\nLevel 2 : Head ->................ -> {\"Robin\" : 950} -> ...... -> {\"Carl\" : 500} -> Null\nLevel 3 : Head -> {\"Alice\" : 1800} -> {\"Robin\" : 950} -> {\"Duke\" : 700} -> {\"Carl\" : 500} -> Null\n\nLane 3 can be used to find out the closest match and then depending on the next element, the search can go to Level 2 and then\nto Level 3, thus reducing linear lookups and improving the complexity to Binary Search level of O(log N).",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Redis in the ZSET implementation uses a combination of HashMap and a skip list.\nFor direct lookups of a player and their score, we can use the Hashmap and answer their score.\n\nBut to identify the rank, we need to do sorting and find the K th largest element. To prevent compute on the go, Redis ZSET \ninternally uses Skip Lists.\n\nA skip list would maintain the elements with three lanes : \n\nLevel 3 : Head -> ..........................................................  -> {\"Carl\" : 500} -> Null\nLevel 2 : Head ->................ -> {\"Robin\" : 950} -> ...... -> {\"Carl\" : 500} -> Null\nLevel 3 : Head -> {\"Alice\" : 1800} -> {\"Robin\" : 950} -> {\"Duke\" : 700} -> {\"Carl\" : 500} -> Null\n\nLane 3 can be used to find out the closest match and then depending on the next element, the search can go to Level 2 and then\nto Level 3, thus reducing linear lookups and improving the complexity to Binary Search level of O(log N).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Uq2YVKMh1v5KHP7YNXyRP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 698.6666666666665,
      "y": 1012.6666666666669,
      "width": 6.400000095367432,
      "height": 20,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
