    },
    {
      "id": "xLG6Mh9sAZt5_SmItBb9e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1346.8411458333333,
      "y": 1392.6184895833335,
      "width": 365.95196533203125,
      "height": 320,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6d",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 422480435,
      "version": 639,
      "versionNonce": 1917060819,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777243465637,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Score Process workers can consume those\nevents and update the Redis ZSets and \nfinally update to our SQL database.\nThis helps decoupling ingestion from \nprocessing.\n\nInstead of maybe seeing 10M writes / sec\nRedis might see 200K writes / sec, as we \nhave grouped them together in our in memory\nbuffer. \n\nStability : if the game has massive spike in \ntraffic, Kafka can absorb the shock and can\nupdate Redis in a steady speed based on how \nfast the Score Process workers pull messages\nout of Kafka.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Score Process workers can consume those\nevents and update the Redis ZSets and \nfinally update to our SQL database.\nThis helps decoupling ingestion from \nprocessing.\n\nInstead of maybe seeing 10M writes / sec\nRedis might see 200K writes / sec, as we \nhave grouped them together in our in memory\nbuffer. \n\nStability : if the game has massive spike in \ntraffic, Kafka can absorb the shock and can\nupdate Redis in a steady speed based on how \nfast the Score Process workers pull messages\nout of Kafka.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2iFwkOcgAaSvXuIjKokIk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 30.333333333333258,
      "y": 2115.8333333333335,
      "width": 238.5400390625,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6e",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 179681277,
      "version": 31,
      "versionNonce": 1070601587,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777243524734,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Handling the leaderboard",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Handling the leaderboard",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "O3Ixm2FKRogUYVuU3rztU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 33.33333333333326,
      "y": 2158.2122395833335,
      "width": 926.7041015625,
      "height": 520,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6f",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 593249267,
      "version": 1250,
      "versionNonce": 406906717,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777244040517,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A single Redis instance cannot hold 100M users, so we would have to shard.\nThere are two approaches here : \n\n1. Fixed Range Partitioning (Tiered) : \nThe approach here is to divide the shards into score ranges : \nShard 1 : Score 0 - 1000\nShard 2 : Score 1001 - 2000\nShard 3 : Score 2001 - 3000 till infinity\n\nPros : \n- Easier to find the top K, just query the latest shard, would contain the players with highest score\n\nCons : \n- if everyone is a beginner, one shard would contain all the records as everybody is in a range of 0 - 1000 for example.\n\n\n2. Hash partitioning (Scatter Gather approach) : \nHere we hash the specific id and update in the corresponding shard\n\nPros : \nData is more evenly distributed\n\nCons : \nTo get top K scores, you will have to query all the shards and get data out of them and do the computation.\nFor N shards, we will have to read the K entries from each shard.\nLeaderboard Service will have to merge the N lists and find the top K from among them and return.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A single Redis instance cannot hold 100M users, so we would have to shard.\nThere are two approaches here : \n\n1. Fixed Range Partitioning (Tiered) : \nThe approach here is to divide the shards into score ranges : \nShard 1 : Score 0 - 1000\nShard 2 : Score 1001 - 2000\nShard 3 : Score 2001 - 3000 till infinity\n\nPros : \n- Easier to find the top K, just query the latest shard, would contain the players with highest score\n\nCons : \n- if everyone is a beginner, one shard would contain all the records as everybody is in a range of 0 - 1000 for example.\n\n\n2. Hash partitioning (Scatter Gather approach) : \nHere we hash the specific id and update in the corresponding shard\n\nPros : \nData is more evenly distributed\n\nCons : \nTo get top K scores, you will have to query all the shards and get data out of them and do the computation.\nFor N shards, we will have to read the K entries from each shard.\nLeaderboard Service will have to merge the N lists and find the top K from among them and return.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
